# What Tutors is

### A generator, a reader, and the format between them

Tutors Ladder, Orientation, talk 1

---

## Two components

Tutors is a set of open source components for building learning experiences on open web standards.
Two of them matter most:

- **Generator**: turns a folder of learning content into a Tutors course
- **Reader**: presents that course as a web experience you can browse

Source: the README of `tutors-sdk/tutors-reference-course`.

---

## One file joins them

```
my-course/  --generator-->  json/tutors.json  --reader-->  pages
```

- `tutors.json` is the whole course: every topic, lab, talk and note, with the Markdown already inlined
- The reader never reads a Markdown file from disk; it fetches `tutors.json`
- The types live in `packages/jsr/model/src/types/learning-objects.ts`; the top one is `Course`

Source: `docs/COURSE-PAGE-WALKTHROUGH.md`, section 3.

---

## The course format

A course is a folder. Folder names start with the kind of thing they hold.

| Folder or file | Becomes |
| --- | --- |
| `course.md`, `properties.yaml` | the course and its settings |
| `topic-*` | a topic card |
| `unit-*`, `side-*` | a group of cards inside a topic |
| `talk-*` | slides: a PDF or a Marp deck |
| `book-*` | a lab: steps named `00.Title.md`, `01.Title.md`, ... |
| `note-*`, `web-*`, `archive-*` | a page, a link, a download |

The full list of kinds is `simpleTypes` in `packages/jsr/model/src/types/type-utils.ts`.

---

## Folder names are the contract

- The generator reads the kind from the folder name
- A `book-*` folder is published as a `lab`
- A folder whose name has no kind is left out
- So a typo is not an error to the generator: your content just vanishes

That is why there is a validator. `tutors validate` reports rules V001 to V021, such as V002: "Every folder
that holds course material has a name that starts with a learning-object kind".

Source: `packages/jsr/course-rules/src/finding.ts`.

---

## The upstream repositories

| Repository | What it holds |
| --- | --- |
| `tutors-sdk/tutors-mono-repo` | the generator, the reader, four more apps and every shared package |
| `tutors-sdk/tutors-reference-course` | a course that uses every learning-object kind |
| `tutors-sdk/tutors-release-harness` | runs a release candidate beside production and fails on any unclaimed difference |

This course pins each to one commit. `tutors/LADDER.md` lists them.

---

## The mono-repo today: apps

Five apps under `apps/`, all SvelteKit:

| Directory | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `apps/reader` | the course reader behind tutors.dev |
| `apps/catalogue` | course catalogue |
| `apps/live` | live classroom and presence |
| `apps/time` | student activity and time tracking |
| `apps/validator` | published course and local ZIP validation |

Source: README.md, "Applications".

---

## The mono-repo today: packages

- **11** folders under `packages/jsr/`: Deno packages, most published to JSR, such as `model`, `gen`,
  `tutors`, `tutors-lite`, `course-rules`, `identity`, `realtime`
- **21** Svelte packages under `packages/svelte/` (each with a `package.json`), from `runes` and `course` to
  `ui-primitives`, `ui-navigators` and `ui-components`
- One pnpm workspace: `pnpm-workspace.yaml` lists `packages/jsr/*`, `packages/svelte/*`,
  `packages/svelte/utils/*` and `apps/*`

---

## The mono-repo today: tests and workflows

| Thing | Count at the pinned commit |
| --- | --- |
| folders under `tests/` | 19, from `architecture` and `bdd` to `security` and `release` |
| test files (`*.test.*`, `*.spec.*`) | about 220 |
| BDD feature files in `tests/bdd/features` | 85 |
| distinct EARS Rule ids (`@rule-NNNN`) | over 400 |
| GitHub workflows in `.github/workflows` | 17 |

Counted from the clone with `ls` and `git ls-files`. Recount when you pull: it grows.

---

## What it takes to run

- Node.js 26.13.0 or later (`engines` in `package.json`; `.nvmrc` pins it)
- pnpm 11.24.0 (`packageManager` in `package.json`)
- Deno at the version in `.dvmrc` (2.9.6), for `packages/jsr/`
- `cp .env.example .env` sets `PUBLIC_ANON_MODE=TRUE`: no Supabase project or OAuth app needed

Then `pnpm dev` and open `http://localhost:5173/course/reference-course`.

---

## Why that is a lot to take in

- Two runtimes: pnpm and Node for the apps, Deno for `packages/jsr/`
- A layered architecture with dependency rules that fail the build
- Hundreds of EARS Rules, mutation floors, coverage floors that work both ways
- A release that a separate harness must accept

All of it is there for a reason. None of it is where a newcomer should start.

---

## Where this course starts instead

- Rung 1 is a few hundred lines of TypeScript with no dependencies: a course folder in, `tutors.json` out
- Each rung adds one capability and the expertise it needs
- At the Summit you read the real repository and recognise every part

Next: the capability ladder.
